  "section": "Decision guide",
  "titleRegister": "claim",
  "contents": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Rolling is the efficient default; blue-green buys clean reversal, while canary buys evidence before promotion."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "The table shows a clear trade-off: rolling updates reuse the existing Deployment pattern and usually need the least spare capacity, blue-green keeps both versions available for a simpler full switch, and canary limits exposure while demanding more operational control.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Choose among them based on blast-radius tolerance, spare capacity, observability, and rollback urgency; this comparison assumes Kubernetes Service plus ingress only, with no service mesh required.",
          "bullet": false
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Use the comparison to frame the decision as a trade-off rather than a universal ranking. Rolling updates are the pragmatic starting point when capacity is constrained; blue-green is attractive when rapid, clean reversal matters most; canary is strongest when the team can observe incremental exposure and act on evidence before promotion."
}

{
  "slideIndex": 2,
  "section": "Strategy mechanics",
  "titleRegister": "claim",
  "contents": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Rolling updates keep spare capacity low, but rollback still unwinds a partially exposed release."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Deployment controls replace pods incrementally while the active Service routes traffic across old and new replicas, making rolling updates a lower-cost default than blue-green or canary when limited surge capacity is acceptable.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Readiness gates, minReadySeconds, progress deadlines, and revision history contain rollout risk, but reverting to a prior ReplicaSet or Deployment revision may require reversing changes already seen by users; the pattern therefore fits routine releases only when health checks are strong.",
          "bullet": false
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "The key trade-off is operational rather than purely infrastructural: rolling updates avoid maintaining a full parallel environment, but they expose the change incrementally through the same Service. Safeguards determine whether an unhealthy revision stops before wider exposure; rollback restores the prior revision but does not erase effects already produced during partial exposure."
}

