        {
          "text": "Readiness gates, minReadySeconds, progress deadlines, and revision history constrain the rollout, but a rollback restores a prior ReplicaSet only after users may already have seen the new version; this makes rolling updates best suited to routine releases with strong health checks and acceptable incremental exposure.",
          "bullet": false
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "The diagram and comparison table together show that rolling updates trade immediate traffic isolation for lower spare-capacity cost and continuous Service routing. Rollback returns the Deployment to a prior revision, but it does not erase exposure that has already occurred or instantly reverse every in-flight request. Source: Kubernetes Deployment documentation."
}

{
  "slideIndex": 3,
  "section": "Strategy mechanics",
  "titleRegister": "claim",
  "contents": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Blue-green trades gradual exposure for a clean, reversible cutover"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "The new version runs beside the current version as a separate environment or workload, then Kubernetes Service or ingress routing moves traffic to green only after validation; because both versions must remain available, spare-capacity cost is high.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Rollback is operationally simple—route traffic back to blue—but the full population is exposed at once, so this fits releases requiring a clear boundary, rapid reversal, or environment-level validation rather than gradual user exposure; this comparison assumes Kubernetes Service plus ingress, with no service mesh.",
          "bullet": false
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Blue-green creates a strong release boundary by keeping the current and new workloads independently available during validation. The operating model is intentionally limited to Kubernetes Service and ingress routing, so no service mesh is needed for the decision described here. Emphasize that rollback is a routing change, while validation quality remains critical because cutover exposes the full population at once. Routing and rollback framing aligns with Kubernetes, Argo Rollouts, and Flagger documentation."
}

